Cycles: build the SSE4.2 CPU kernel on powerpc64le through the compiler's
SSE-on-VSX compatibility headers.

Cycles has an SSE kernel (selected by __KERNEL_SSE__..__KERNEL_SSE42__ in
util/optimization.h) and compiles it on ARM through sse2neon.  On POWER
nothing set those macros, so every kernel fell back to scalar code.  GCC
(>= 8) and clang ship <xmmintrin.h> .. <nmmintrin.h> for powerpc64le that
implement SSE through SSE4.2 on VSX (POWER8 and later), gated behind
-DNO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS.  This is the same shape as the sse2neon path and
the same technique Embree was ported with (packages/embree).

- intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt: probe the headers (SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD),
  then take a POWER branch beside the NEON one: no SSE/AVX compiler flags,
  -DWITH_SSE2VSX -DNO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS.
- util/optimization.h: __KERNEL_VSX__ + the four SSE macros under
  __powerpc64__ && __VSX__ && WITH_SSE2VSX.
- util/simd.h: include the compatibility headers instead of <x86intrin.h> /
  <sse2neon.h>; provide _mm_dp_ps, the one intrinsic Cycles uses that the
  headers lack; bit scans through __builtin_ctz/clz on any GCC/clang target
  instead of the 32/64-iteration loops (the loop fallback also has a
  `1 << bit` int shift in bitscan(uint64_t)).
- device/cpu/device.cpp: report "VSX" in `_cycles.system_info()` so the
  active kernel is visible at runtime.

Verified on POWER9 (see packages/blender/README.md): kernel.cpp compiles
with GCC 16 with no other changes (no -flax-vector-conversions needed);
the object goes from 43k to 111k VSX/VMX instructions with xvmulsp/xvaddsp
vector forms replacing the scalar xsmulsp/xsaddsp; renders match the scalar
build.

Portability fix, worth sending upstream (projects.blender.org/blender/blender).

diff -ruN -x obj a/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt b/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt	2026-02-09 09:34:40.000000000 -0700
+++ b/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt	2026-09-10 21:54:11.229522256 -0600
@@ -30,6 +30,21 @@
 # TODO: this code could be refactored a bit to avoid duplication.
 # NOTE: CXX_HAS_SSE42 is needed in case passing SSE flags fails altogether (`gcc-arm`).
 
+# powerpc64le: does the compiler ship the SSE-on-VSX compatibility headers?
+# (GCC >= 8 and clang do; they refuse to compile unless NO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS
+# acknowledges the port.)
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "^(ppc64le|powerpc64le)$" AND NOT DEFINED SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD)
+  include(CheckCXXSourceCompiles)
+  check_cxx_source_compiles(
+    "#define NO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS 1
+     #include <nmmintrin.h>
+     int main() {
+       __m128 a = _mm_set1_ps(1.0f);
+       return _mm_movemask_ps(_mm_cmpeq_ps(a, a)) == 0xf ? 0 : 1;
+     }"
+    SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD)
+endif()
+
 # Determine vectorization support and required build flags
 if(WITH_CYCLES_NATIVE_ONLY)
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
@@ -73,6 +88,15 @@
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
   set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 FALSE)
   set(CXX_HAS_F16C FALSE)
+elseif(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "^(ppc64le|powerpc64le)$" AND SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD)
+  # On POWER the compiler's SSE-on-VSX compatibility headers (xmmintrin.h ..
+  # nmmintrin.h) compile the cycles SSE kernels into native VSX instructions,
+  # the same way SSE2NEON does on ARM.  There are no SSE or AVX compiler
+  # flags here; util/optimization.h selects the kernel on WITH_SSE2VSX.
+  set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
+  set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 FALSE)
+  set(CXX_HAS_F16C FALSE)
+  add_definitions(-DWITH_SSE2VSX -DNO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS)
 elseif(WIN32 AND MSVC AND NOT CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "Clang")
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 TRUE)
   set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 TRUE)
